  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 112 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nLittle change in the overall forecast thinking across the region \nover the next 7 days. Northwesterly flow continues across the Upper \nMidwest and Great Lakes Region. A shortwave is forecast to move\ninto the Great Lakes Tuesday night through Wednesday night with\nthe better forcing to our north and east. 25 to 45 percent \nchance of showers and storms across the area is now mainly on \nWednesday morning. High temperature on Wednesday will warm into \nthe lower to mid 80s with dewpoints in the mid to upper 60s\nresulting in CAPE around 500 to 1500 J/KG and 0 to 6 km shear \naround 30 knots may result in isolated severe storms. \n\nHigh pressure is forecast to build into area for the second \nhalf of the week. The flow aloft becomes more progressive late \nthis week into next weekend as a storm system digs into the \nPacific Northwest and flattens the Plains ridge. This has been \ndelayed from previous forecasts until the weekend. However a \nsubtle wave Saturday will bring the potential for another round \nof showers and storms during the morning with a another \npotential round of showers and storms Sunday into Monday. \nDespite POPs each period Saturday through Monday, there were \nwill be a lot of dry hours with the broad-brushed Pops being \ndue to timing differences between different operational models \nand their ensembles. Temperatures gradually increase from the \nlower 80s on Thursday to widespread mid to upper 80s on Sunday\nand Monday.",
